Compile a program to a backend text form

Usage: redextape emit [OPTIONS] --lang <LANG> <PATH>

Arguments:
  <PATH>
          The program to compile. `-` reads standard input

Options:
      --config <PATH>
          Read settings from this file instead of searching for one. Naming a file that does not exist is an error, where finding no file during the search is not

      --lang <LANG>
          Which text form to write. All three read back — `tm` through `parse_tm_full`, `lambda` through `parse_lambda`, `asm` through `parse_asm`. `tm` and `asm` are also executable from the command line: `redextape run` takes an emitted `.tm` or `.asm` file directly

          Possible values:
          - tm:     A complete self-describing Turing machine, header included, that `redextape run` executes
          - lambda: The λ-calculus lowering of the program, which `parse_lambda` and the editor grammar read
          - asm:    The register-machine lowering, read back by `parse_asm` and executable by `redextape run`

      --encoding <ENCODING>
          Tape encoding, defaulting to unary. `--lang tm` only: passing it with any other target is an error rather than a silent no-op. `binary` packs a far larger value into the same field width, so it can express programs unary refuses

          Possible values:
          - unary:  One cell per unit. The default, and the narrower of the two: the widest field there is holds values up to 63 (measured, not supposed)
          - binary: Packed digits. Holds a far larger value in the same field, so it expresses programs unary refuses

      --no-config
          Ignore any `redextape.toml` and use built-in defaults

      --field-width <CELLS>
          TM tape field width in cells, overriding `emit.field-width` in `redextape.toml`. The same values that key accepts: 4..=64 to pin a width, or 0 to auto-fit — which is also what omitting the flag means, a search that starts narrow and widens until the values fit. Anything else exits 2. `--lang tm` only: passing it with any other target is an error rather than a silent no-op

  -o, --out <OUT>
          Write here instead of standard output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
